## 3. Retained dominant outer mass exceeds `2.21904D`

Let `z` be retained dominant outer-occurrence mass and `p` the retained complement O--O pair mass. The audited retained-mass/complement-incidence system gives

\[
z+p\ge\pi-{t\over2}-x-1.05h-o(1),
\tag{E70.12}
\]

\[
p\le
{1.05^2h^2+\delta-F(z)\over2(1-\delta)}+o(1)
\tag{E70.13}
\]

on the energy-feasible side.

Assume `z<=Z=2.21904`. The capped left side is increasing in `z`, with certified derivative above `0.96`, so it is enough to use `z=Z`.

Put

\[
h_*=R_T-2F(Z).
\]

The exact enclosure gives

\[
0<h_*<H-2\times10^{-6}.
\tag{E70.14}
\]

On the boundary `delta=F(Z)` one has `x=h_*-h`. The capped survivor excess is increasing in `h`, so the adverse point is `h=h_*,x=0`. Exact outward evaluation gives

\[
\boxed{
Z+{1.05^2h_*^2\over2(1-F(Z))}
-\pi+{T\over2}+1.05h_*
<-2\times10^{-5}.
}
\tag{E70.15}
\]

For `delta<F(Z)`, the parent negative `Q_x,Q_h` derivatives move the adverse point back to the same boundary. Therefore

\[
\boxed{z>2.21904-o(1).}
\tag{E70.16}
\]

