## 4. Every exact phase retains more than `0.6904D`

For one exact phase atom, let

\[
f(s)=s-2\sin(s/2).
\]

The phasewise one-well decomposition gives

\[
\delta\ge f(z_C)+f(z_U)+f(z_V)-o(1).
\tag{E70.17}
\]

Suppose one phase retained at most

\[
A=0.6904.
\]

Since `A<Z/3`, convexity makes the cheapest other two phase masses equal. Therefore

\[
f(z_C)+f(z_U)+f(z_V)
\ge
f(A)+2f\left({Z-A\over2}\right)-o(1).
\]

The exact verifier proves that the right side exceeds the complete detour ceiling in (E70.11) by more than

\[
2.1\times10^{-6}.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{z_C,z_U,z_V>0.6904-o(1).}
\tag{E70.18}
\]

In particular,

\[
z_V\le2V+o(1)
\]

implies the independent lower bound

\[
\boxed{V>0.3452-o(1).}
\tag{E70.19}
\]

This lower bound is used to certify the strong physical pair branch without circularly replacing the actual larger leaf by its contradiction threshold.

