## 5. Both leaf train masses are below `0.5436D`

Assume for contradiction

\[
U\ge U_0:=0.5436.
\]

Use

\[
W_0=1.912984,
\qquad
E_0=0.90689955.
\]

Put

\[
C_{low}=W_0-2U_0=0.825784.
\]

For `C<=C_low`, generic center capacity is increasing and satisfies

\[
C(W_0-C)<E_0-0.009.
\]

For

\[
C\ge1.046,
\]

generic center capacity is decreasing and satisfies

\[
C(W_0-C)<E_0-3\times10^{-5}.
\]

It remains to treat

\[
0.825784<C<1.046.
\tag{E70.20}
\]

The repaired strong-branch scope test applies **before** replacing `U` by `U_0`. From (E70.19),

\[
C+U=W-V
<W_0-{A\over2}+o(1).
\]

Since `U>=U_0`,

\[
{(C+U-K)_+\over U}
\le
{W_0-A/2-K_-\over U_0}+o(1),
\qquad K={2\over\sqrt3}.
\]

The exact certificate gives

\[
\boxed{
{W_0-A/2-K_-\over U_0}<0.760
<\sqrt{20/29}.
}
\tag{E70.21}
\]

Moreover `C<1.046<K`; for fixed `C<K`, the excess ratio is nondecreasing in the leaf mass. Since `V<=U`, the smaller leaf ratio is no larger. Thus the physical `1/10` pair branch is valid for both center/leaf pairs on the original configuration.

Only after this scope check do we make the adverse replacements `W->W_0` and `U->U_0`. Set

\[
V=W_0-C-U_0
\]

and

\[
\begin{aligned}
G(C)=&\ C(W_0-C)\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{V\over C}\right)(C+U_0-K)^2\\
&-{1\over10}\left(1-{U_0\over C}\right)(C+V-K)^2.
\end{aligned}
\tag{E70.22}
\]

The exact derivative identity gives `G''<-2` throughout (E70.20). At

\[
c_0=0.9301,
\]

the tangent bound gives

\[
\max G
\le G(c_0)+{G'(c_0)^2\over4}
<E_0-4.2\times10^{-6}.
\tag{E70.23}
\]

This contradicts (E70.10). Therefore

\[
\boxed{U,V<0.5436-o(1).}
\tag{E70.24}
\]

