## 6. Each leaf retains more than `0.1076D` good O--O pair weight

For phase `i`, the retained terminal inequality gives

\[
z_i\le M_i+P_i+o(1),
\]

where `P_i` is retained O--O pair weight. Equations (E70.18) and (E70.24) yield

\[
\boxed{P_U,P_V>0.6904-0.5436-o(1)=0.1468-o(1).}
\tag{E70.25}
\]

Call a retained O--O train bad when its center crossing degree is at most half of the center family. The hostile-audited center/leaf correlation bounds total bad weight across both leaves by

\[
{2[C(W-C)-E_{dom}]\over C-U}+o(1).
\]

With the adverse constants `W_0,E_0,U_0`, the assertion that this is below

\[
B=0.0392
\]

reduces to a quadratic in `C`. The exact discriminant satisfies

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{disc}<-3.0\times10^{-4}.}
\tag{E70.26}
\]

Thus, even if the entire bad weight is charged to one leaf, each leaf retains good O--O weight

\[
\boxed{G_U,G_V>0.1468-0.0392-o(1)=0.1076-o(1).}
\tag{E70.27}
\]

Every good `U` train and good `V` train cross more than half of the same center family, so they share a center neighbor. Triangle-freeness makes every good cross-leaf pair noncrossing.

---

## 7. Keep the leaf remainder bathtub instead of discarding it

Fix `g<0.1076` and trim each good weighted family to total pair weight exactly `g`. The paired-remainder lemma applies because the two leaf phases differ by `pi/3` and the complete cross-product is noncrossing.

The paired terminal submeasure in each leaf has occurrence mass exactly `2g`. Since the full retained occurrence mass is at least `A`, the nonnegative remainder has mass at least

\[
A-2g.
\]

The two trimmed pair energies satisfy

\[
E_U^g+E_V^g
\ge(2-\sqrt3)g-o(1),
\]

while the two remainders pay

\[
2f(A-2g)-o(1).
\]

The center phase, which is disjoint from both leaf supports, pays `f(A)-o(1)`. Therefore

\[
\delta
\ge
f(A)+(2-\sqrt3)g+2f(A-2g)-o(1).
\tag{E70.28}
\]

Let `g\uparrow0.1076`. Then

\[
A-2g\downarrow0.4752.
\]

Using the safe bound `sqrt(3)<1.7321`, the exact certificate gives

\[
f(0.6904)>0.01363022,
\]

\[
(2-\sqrt3)(0.1076)>0.02882604,
\]

and

\[
2f(0.4752)>0.00891706.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{\delta>0.05137333-o(1).}
\tag{E70.29}
\]

But (E70.11) gives

\[
\delta<0.05056604+o(1).
\]

The exact contradiction margin is more than

\[
\boxed{8.0\times10^{-4}.}
\tag{E70.30}
\]

Therefore no full-core Type-II survivor exists with

\[
t\le1.84370.
\]

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The gain from `1.84365` to `1.84370` is not produced by another atomic-gap decimal. It comes from restoring selected occurrence energy that the previous leaf-pair ledger discarded.

The current full-core survivor strip is reduced to

\[
1.84370\lesssim t\le1.844.
\]

The paired-remainder ledger still has substantial final energy slack at `1.84370`; the next bottlenecks are again the residual/phase localization and the leaf-mass capacity threshold. A tighter propagation may reach a somewhat larger endpoint, but further numerical work should be compared against the independently merged exact finite multishear theorem, which attacks the same macroscopic O--O family structurally.

**Disposition:** proof-only full-core floor raised from `1.84365` to `1.84370`. C-II and Level I remain open.